      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "North Face (2008) stands among the greatest mountain climbing films ever made, bringing raw intensity to the true story of two German climbers attempting the Eiger's north face in 1936. Like <a href=\"/movie/11194\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"11194\">Touching the Void</a> (2003), it captures the brutal reality of alpine climbing without Hollywood glamorization. Director Philipp Stölzl crafts a masterclass in sustained tension, letting the mountain's deadly reputation loom over every frame. The film's attention to period detail - from the wooden ice axes to wool climbing gear - creates an immersive pre-war atmosphere that <a href=\"/movie/49009\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"49009\">The Way Back</a> (2010) and <a href=\"/movie/253412\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"253412\">Everest</a> (2015) would later echo."
      },
