      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The golden age of mountaineering films began with <a href=\"/movie/217268\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"217268\">The Epic of Everest</a> (1924), documenting Mallory's fatal attempt. But it was <a href=\"/movie/89571\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"89571\">Five Days One Summer</a> (1982) that first captured the psychological warfare between climber and mountain in dramatic form. <a href=\"/movie/11678\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"11678\">Vertical Limit</a> (2000) went pure Hollywood with its impossible stunts, while <a href=\"/movie/44115\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"44115\">127 Hours</a> (2010) returned to gritty realism. North Face bridges these approaches - historically accurate yet cinematically gripping."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "What sets North Face apart is its political subtext. Set against the rise of Nazi Germany, the climb becomes propaganda fodder, with the regime desperate for an \"Aryan victory\" on the infamous mountainface. This adds layers of meaning found in other German films of the era like <a href=\"/movie/613\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"613\">Downfall</a> (2004) and Sophie Scholl (2005). The mountain becomes both a literal challenge and metaphor for destructive nationalist ambition. SUBHEAD: The Technical Achievement"
      },
